"""Week46 Days 5-6: imbalance, ensembles and a traceable final experiment."""

L(4, '1. Measure imbalance and positive-event support first',
  'Count positives before selecting metrics, splits or model remedies.',
  [('Class imbalance; event support', 'Imbalance means some actual labels are much less common than others. Count each class and the number of positive events in every allowed evaluation partition.'),
   ('Natural prevalence', 'The original class mixture of the evaluated population. Artificially balancing evaluation data changes the precision and accuracy question being measured.')],
  'A class ratio alone does not decide how hard a task is. Feature overlap, label quality and the number of independent positive examples also matter. A thousand rows with only a few positive outcomes can provide much less positive-event evidence than the total suggests.\n\nStratification helps retain class proportions but cannot create new events. Repeating a rare row adds fitting weight, not independent information. A tiny positive denominator also means one missed event can move recall substantially. Keep those counts beside any rounded percentage.\n\nThe main project’s simulator deliberately creates rare cancellations. Inspect the training mix and preserve validation/test at their original mixture. Remedies such as weights, resampling and threshold changes need a clear purpose; there is no requirement that training classes must always be made50/50. Do not use an artificial evaluation balance to make a precision result look more attractive.',
  'from lab import make_splits\nd = make_splits()\ny = d["y_train"]\nprint("Training counts:", y.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())\nprint("Training positive prevalence:", round(float(y.mean()), 4))\nprint("Majority share:", round(float(1 - y.mean()), 4))\nprint("Count independent observed outcomes, not only total rows")',
  'Show the recall change caused by one additional found event when there are12positives.',
  'positives = 12\nprint("Recall with2found:", round(2 / positives, 3))\nprint("Recall with3found:", round(3 / positives, 3))\nprint("One-event change:", round(1 / positives, 3))',
  'Compare row count and positive support for two invented datasets.',
  'datasets = {"A": {"rows": 1000, "positive": 10}, "B": {"rows": 200, "positive": 40}}\nfor name, counts in datasets.items():\n    print(name, "rows", counts["rows"], "positive evidence", counts["positive"])',
  [('Does stratification create new independent positive events?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a fixed seed', 'It assigns existing observations to partitions.'),
   ('Should validation be artificially balanced just to improve a displayed precision?', 'No', 'Yes', 'Only for logistic regression', 'Changing evaluation prevalence changes what precision means.')],
  'Report only total rows and assume a balanced evaluation queue represents the original population.', 'Report event support and preserve the intended evaluation mixture.',
  'The training count and positive share describe this simulator only. The twelve-event arithmetic shows why a small actual-positive denominator requires careful interpretation.')

L(4, '2. Use class weights as a fitting choice',
  'Compute balanced weights and assess weighted predictions on natural validation data.',
  [('class_weight="balanced"', 'For this classifier, balanced weights are proportional to n_samples/(n_classes*class_count). They change the contribution of training examples to the fitting objective.'),
   ('Weighted scores and calibration', 'Changing the fitting objective can change probability scale as well as decisions. Weighted predict_proba outputs are not automatically calibrated probabilities under the original prevalence.')],
  'Class weights make each rare training event count more strongly in the objective. This is different from collecting more outcomes or changing the validation distribution. It can help a model pay attention to rare positives, but does not guarantee improved precision, cost or calibrated probability.\n\nThe balanced formula gives each class the same total training weight in this binary setting. It does not make the observed training rows independent duplicates or establish that the chosen weights match real decision costs. The main project treats balanced logistic as one declared candidate, then assesses its score/threshold pair on validation.\n\nCompare weighted and unweighted models on exactly the same natural-prevalence validation rows. Starting with0.5makes the change visible, but the same numeric threshold can have a different meaning after weighting. Later threshold selection uses validation scores rather than assuming the weighted output is directly calibrated population risk.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates, positive_scores, binary_metrics\nd = make_splits()\ncounts = d["y_train"].value_counts().sort_index()\nweights = {int(label): len(d["y_train"]) / (2 * int(count)) for label, count in counts.items()}\nprint("Balanced training weights:", {k: round(v, 3) for k, v in weights.items()})\nfor name in ["logistic", "balanced_logistic"]:\n    m = make_candidates()[name].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\n    r = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], positive_scores(m, d["X_valid"]), 0.5)\n    print(name, "FP", r["fp"], "FN", r["fn"], "precision", round(r["precision"], 3), "recall", round(r["recall"], 3))',
  'Compute balanced class weights for90negatives and10positives.',
  'counts = {0: 90, 1: 10}\nweights = {label: 100 / (2 * n) for label, n in counts.items()}\nprint(weights)\nprint("Weighted class totals:", {label: counts[label] * weight for label, weight in weights.items()})',
  'Confirm that weighting leaves the number of actual validation cases unchanged.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates\nd = make_splits()\nsize = len(d["y_valid"])\nmake_candidates()["balanced_logistic"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Validation unchanged:", len(d["y_valid"]) == size)',
  [('What do class weights change?', 'The training objective’s example contributions', 'The actual number of observed outcomes', 'The true customer prevalence', 'Weights alter the fit rather than creating new evidence.'),
   ('Are weighted scores automatically calibrated original-population probabilities?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with balanced weights', 'The changed objective needs appropriate natural-prevalence assessment.')],
  'Treat balanced weights as new observations or automatically calibrated personal probabilities.', 'Evaluate the weighted method and its decisions on the intended validation population.',
  'The weight formula is computed from training labels only. The validation rows and0.5rule stay fixed, so changes in error counts reflect the changed fitted candidate.')

L(4, '3. Duplicate only minority training sources',
  'Perform training-only random oversampling and recognise that duplicates add weight, not information.',
  [('Random oversampling; oversample_training', 'The supplied helper randomly duplicates minority training positions until class counts match, preserves each input/label pair and shuffles the resulting training table.'),
   ('Sampling with replacement', 'A source observation may be chosen repeatedly. Repeated copies carry the same observed outcome and are not independent new examples.')],
  'Random oversampling gives the fitting process more copies of minority examples. It can alter the effective class emphasis, but it does not reveal new positive-event patterns. A mislabeled rare row can also receive more influence when copied.\n\nSplit first. Pass only the training portion to the helper, then fit the full pipeline on that resampled training table. Keep validation and final test untouched at their original prevalence. If using cross-validation, repeat resampling inside each fitting fold rather than before constructing the folds.\n\nThe helper resets the resampled row index for fitting, so do not mistake those new row numbers for independent source identities. The source input/label pairs remain copies of original training records. Keep a source audit in a real project. The example compares observed support before and after duplication and evaluates the resulting model on natural validation cases.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates, oversample_training, positive_scores, binary_metrics\nd = make_splits()\nXr, yr = oversample_training(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nm = make_candidates()["logistic"].fit(Xr, yr)\nr = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], positive_scores(m, d["X_valid"]), 0.5)\nprint("Original training counts:", d["y_train"].value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())\nprint("Resampled counts:", yr.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())\nprint("Validation rows:", r["rows"], "FP", r["fp"], "FN", r["fn"])',
  'Verify repeated-source oversampling changes row count without adding new unique outcomes.',
  'source_ids = [0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3]\nprint("Fitting rows:", len(source_ids))\nprint("Unique observed sources:", len(set(source_ids)))',
  'Confirm the helper returns paired resampled tables of equal length.',
  'from lab import make_splits, oversample_training\nd = make_splits()\nX, y = oversample_training(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Paired lengths:", len(X) == len(y))\nprint("Paired indices:", X.index.equals(y.index))',
  [('Does copying a positive row create a new independent positive observation?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if its index changes', 'Source identity and repeated fitting weight are not new outcome evidence.'),
   ('When must oversampling happen relative to a validation split?', 'Inside the allowed training portion', 'Before splitting every row', 'On validation too', 'Copies must not cross into evaluation evidence.')],
  'Oversample the complete dataset before splitting it into training and evaluation.', 'Split original sources first and resample only the current training portion.',
  'The class counts become balanced in the resampled fitting table. Evaluation still contains240original validation cases, not a newly balanced set.')

L(4, '4. Undersampling and source leakage need explicit tradeoffs',
  'Construct a training-only undersample and show why related copies must not cross a split.',
  [('Random undersampling', 'Select fewer majority training rows to change the fitting mix. It reduces available majority information and can increase variability; it is not a free improvement.'),
   ('Duplicate-source leakage', 'Different row numbers can still refer to the same original observation. Keeping copies in both fitting and evaluation makes the evaluation less independent.')],
  'Undersampling keeps all minority training cases and selects a smaller majority subset. It can reduce computation and change class emphasis, but discards observed majority variation. Compare it under the same natural evaluation conditions rather than assuming it helps because the fitting counts are equal.\n\nThe worked example uses positional selections within the existing training table. Both X and y receive the same positions. Validation is neither sampled nor fitted. The challenge isolates duplicate-source leakage using invented source ids, showing why disjoint row positions alone are insufficient.\n\nMore sophisticated methods such as synthetic-neighbour oversampling introduce additional assumptions and libraries. They still belong inside the training boundary and need careful assessment; there is no automatic right to fabricate realistic minority structure. Learn the basic source and evaluation discipline before adding more machinery.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, positive_scores, binary_metrics\nd = make_splits()\ny = d["y_train"].to_numpy()\nminority = np.flatnonzero(y == 1)\nmajority = np.flatnonzero(y == 0)\nrng = np.random.default_rng(46)\npositions = np.r_[minority, rng.choice(majority, len(minority), replace=False)]\nm = make_candidates()["logistic"].fit(d["X_train"].iloc[positions], d["y_train"].iloc[positions])\nr = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], positive_scores(m, d["X_valid"]), 0.5)\nprint("Undersampled rows:", len(positions))\nprint("Unique selected positions:", len(set(positions)))\nprint("Validation precision/recall:", round(r["precision"], 3), round(r["recall"], 3))',
  'Detect a repeated source shared between two otherwise differently numbered row sets.',
  'train_sources = {"A", "B", "C"}\nvalidation_sources = {"C", "D"}\nprint("Source overlap:", sorted(train_sources & validation_sources))',
  'Write a fold-safe resampling order for a future cross-validation exercise.',
  'steps = ["split original sources into folds", "resample current fitting fold", "fit preparation and model", "evaluate untouched validation fold"]\nfor number, step in enumerate(steps, 1):\n    print(number, step)',
  [('What does majority undersampling sacrifice?', 'Some observed majority training information', 'All target leakage automatically', 'The need for a baseline', 'Discarding rows changes the available evidence and can increase variance.'),
   ('Do changed row indices make copied sources independent?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only after shuffling', 'Independence concerns source observations, not their displayed index labels.')],
  'Check only row-index overlap after copying sources and call the evaluation independent.', 'Track source identity and fit resampling inside each allowed training boundary.',
  'The same position vector selects training features and answers. The evaluation portion remains unchanged, while the copied-source exercise exposes a separate leakage risk.')

L(4, '5. Compare three imbalance remedies on one natural validation set',
  'Compare weighting, duplication and threshold selection without altering evaluation prevalence.',
  [('Remedy comparison', 'Weights change fitting loss, duplication changes fitting sample emphasis, and threshold selection changes decisions on existing scores. They are distinct interventions, not interchangeable evidence.'),
   ('Development-only threshold result', 'The threshold here is chosen on the same validation outcomes used to display its result. That is labelled development evidence, not a fresh final evaluation.')],
  'Use the same original training and validation partitions for every method. Start with ordinary logistic at0.5, then compare class-weighted logistic at0.5, training-only oversampled logistic at0.5and the original logistic score vector with its validation-selected threshold.\n\nThe chart reports precision and recall so the tradeoff is visible. It does not declare the highest bar universally best. The chosen objective is illustrative cost, and each remedy can change false alarms, missed events and score reliability differently. No evaluation rows are duplicated or removed.\n\nThe threshold’s displayed score benefited from validation selection, while the fixed0.5methods did not use that selection step. State that difference rather than calling the plot an untouched final comparison. The final project separately freezes its model/threshold pair and reports reserved outcomes, which may disagree with the development ranking.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, positive_scores, binary_metrics, oversample_training, choose_threshold\nd = make_splits()\nmodels = make_candidates()\nbase = models["logistic"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nweighted = models["balanced_logistic"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nXr, yr = oversample_training(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nduplicated = make_candidates()["logistic"].fit(Xr, yr)\np = positive_scores(base, d["X_valid"])\nt = choose_threshold(d["y_valid"], p)["threshold"]\ncases = [("Default", base, 0.5), ("Weighted", weighted, 0.5), ("Duplicate", duplicated, 0.5), ("Threshold", base, t)]\nrows = []\nfor name, model, threshold in cases:\n    r = binary_metrics(d["y_valid"], positive_scores(model, d["X_valid"]), threshold)\n    rows.append(r)\n    print(name, "threshold", round(threshold, 3), "precision", round(r["precision"], 3), "recall", round(r["recall"], 3), "cost", r["total_cost"])\nx = np.arange(len(cases))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.2, 4.1))\nax.bar(x-0.17, [r["precision"] for r in rows], width=0.34, label="Precision", color="#376ea6")\nax.bar(x+0.17, [r["recall"] for r in rows], width=0.34, label="Recall", color="#3d927a")\nax.set(xticks=x, xticklabels=[c[0] for c in cases], ylabel="Validation proportion", ylim=(0, 1.05), title="Same natural validation cases, different remedies")\nax.legend()\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Check that two remedies receive the same evaluation source ids.',
  'from lab import make_splits\nd = make_splits()\nevaluation_for_weights = d["X_valid"].index.tolist()\nevaluation_for_sampling = d["X_valid"].index.tolist()\nprint("Same cases:", evaluation_for_weights == evaluation_for_sampling)',
  'Classify the three remedy types by what they modify.',
  'changes = {"class weights": "training loss contributions", "oversampling": "training source repetitions", "threshold": "decision rule on fitted scores"}\nfor method, change in changes.items():\n    print(method + ": " + change)',
  [('Which remedy can change decisions without refitting the model?', 'Threshold selection', 'Class weighting during fitting', 'Training-only resampling', 'The original score vector can be reused with a new decision rule.'),
   ('Is the selected threshold’s same-validation result a fresh final test?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if precision rises', 'The displayed outcomes also guided that threshold choice.')],
  'Change evaluation prevalence between remedies and compare the resulting precision as though nothing else changed.', 'Keep evaluation cases and their natural mix fixed and name each selection step.',
  'The bars use identical240validation cases. The threshold bar is explicitly development-selected; its apparent gain must still survive a reserved final evaluation.',
  figure=('Precision and recall of a default fit, class weighting, training duplication and threshold selection', 'All methods use the same natural validation cases. Threshold selection uses these validation labels, so its displayed score is development evidence. No universal best method is inferred.'))

L(4, '6. Prevalence shift changes what a flagged score means',
  'Relate precision to class prevalence and preserve honest probability interpretation.',
  [('Prevalence-sensitive precision', 'Even with fixed true- and false-positive rates, precision depends on how common actual positives are. A changed population can change the flagged queue’s reliability.'),
   ('Calibration after weighting or sampling', 'A changed training class mixture or loss can distort population probability interpretation. Reliability assessment and any fitted calibration need a proper development/evaluation boundary.')],
  'Suppose recall is0.8and false-positive rate is0.1. The precision is high when half the population is positive but much lower when only one percent is positive. The same negative-class error rate creates many more false alarms relative to the small event count.\n\nThe formula below assumes those class-conditional rates remain fixed as prevalence changes. That assumption may not hold under a real feature or behaviour shift, so the calculation is a controlled explanation rather than a forecast. It shows why balancing an evaluation table can alter the meaning of its precision result.\n\nCheck original prevalence, positive support and reliability before interpreting probabilities after weighting or resampling. A good ranking, balanced training counts and a fitted sigmoid are not automatic calibration evidence. If thresholds or a calibrator are developed from data, reserve an appropriate independent evaluation for the complete procedure.',
  'tpr, fpr = 0.8, 0.1\nfor prevalence in [0.5, 0.05, 0.01]:\n    precision = prevalence * tpr / (prevalence * tpr + (1 - prevalence) * fpr)\n    print("Prevalence", prevalence, "implied precision", round(precision, 3))\nprint("Assumption: class-conditional rates held fixed")',
  'Compute expectedTPandFPcounts for10000cases at1%prevalence under those fixed rates.',
  'total, prevalence, tpr, fpr = 10000, 0.01, 0.8, 0.1\ntp = total * prevalence * tpr\nfp = total * (1 - prevalence) * fpr\nprint("Expected TP:", tp)\nprint("Expected FP:", fp)\nprint("Implied precision:", round(tp / (tp + fp), 3))',
  'Write three facts to inspect before applying an old threshold to a new population.',
  'for concern in ["feature and time availability", "event prevalence and support", "score reliability and decision costs"]:\n    print(concern)',
  [('Can precision change when prevalence changes under fixed class-conditional rates?', 'Yes', 'Never', 'Only when the threshold changes', 'The base rate changes the relative number of true and false positive decisions.'),
   ('Does balanced training prove calibrated natural-population probabilities?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a logistic model', 'Training emphasis and population reliability are different questions.')],
  'Reuse a precision number from a balanced benchmark as a guarantee for a rare-event population.', 'State the population mix and verify the complete score/decision procedure in the intended setting.',
  'The arithmetic holds recall and false-positive rate fixed while varying prevalence. It isolates a base-rate effect, not evidence that those rates stay unchanged in an actual new population.')

L(5, '1. Random forests average class probabilities',
  'Explain forest classification and distinguish probability averaging from counting hard votes.',
  [('RandomForestClassifier; probability averaging', 'The classifier averages tree class-probability outputs before selecting a class. This is not necessarily the same as a majority vote over each tree’s hard prediction.'),
   ('feature_importances_; impurity summary', 'Forest importances summarise training split improvements. They can favour features with many split values and are not causal effects or guaranteed out-of-sample importance.')],
  'Week45 used a forest to average numeric regression predictions. A classification forest averages per-class probabilities from its fitted trees. Each tree may have mixed-label leaves, so averaging those probabilities can differ from merely counting which class each tree would choose.\n\nThe project uses a declared tree count, depth and minimum leaf size, fitted only on the original training portion. No feature scaling is needed by this tree pipeline, but median preparation remains inside its fit boundary. More trees do not fix target leakage, unreliable labels or a shifted population.\n\nThe worked example verifies the average on transformed validation inputs. Use the pipeline’s preparation rather than bypassing it with raw future values. Training impurity importance can help inspect how the fitted model split the data, but it needs qualification; later permutation assessment uses development outcomes and also has correlation limitations.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, positive_scores\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["forest"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nforest = m.named_steps["model"]\nrows = d["X_valid"].iloc[:3]\nprepared = m[:-1].transform(rows)\naverage = np.mean([tree.predict_proba(prepared) for tree in forest.estimators_], axis=0)\nprint("Tree count:", len(forest.estimators_))\nprint("Probability average matches:", bool(np.allclose(average, m.predict_proba(rows))))\nprint("Positive scores:", positive_scores(m, rows).round(3).tolist())',
  'List the fitted forest’s training impurity summaries with their feature names.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["forest"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nfor name, importance in zip(FEATURES, m.named_steps["model"].feature_importances_):\n    print(name, round(float(importance), 3))',
  'Construct tree probabilities whose average disagrees with a majority of hard tree labels.',
  'import numpy as np\npositive_probabilities = np.array([0.51, 0.51, 0.01])\nprint("Hard positive votes:", int((positive_probabilities > 0.5).sum()), "of3")\nprint("Mean positive probability:", round(float(positive_probabilities.mean()), 3))\nprint("Average-probability positive decision:", bool(positive_probabilities.mean() > 0.5))',
  [('Does this forest necessarily equal majority voting over hard tree labels?', 'No; it averages class probabilities', 'Always', 'Only for two classes', 'Mixed-probability leaves can make the two procedures disagree.'),
   ('Does the highest impurity importance prove a causal cancellation driver?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if the value is large', 'It describes a training split summary within the fitted model.')],
  'Describe forest classification as hard voting only and treat importances as causal facts.', 'Check the actual probability aggregation and qualify each interpretation.',
  'Each tree receives the same pipeline-prepared rows. The equality directly verifies probability averaging; the invented three-tree example shows why hard votes can tell a different story.')

L(5, '2. Gradient boosting improves an additive classification score',
  'Read staged probability predictions and keep stage selection away from final labels.',
  [('GradientBoostingClassifier; binary log-loss boosting', 'The classifier adds successive fitted corrections to an additive decision score. Under this binary loss, probabilities come from a logistic link rather than a simple mean of tree probabilities.'),
   ('staged_predict_proba', 'This iterator exposes probability predictions after successive boosting stages. It supports development inspection; selecting a stage from final-test performance would use the final answers.')],
  'A boosting classifier is not a random forest with a different name. It builds successive corrections to a current score rather than averaging independently varied tree probabilities. Learning rate, stage count and tree depth together control its flexibility.\n\nThe example fits the declared training-only boosting pipeline and prints validation ranking summaries after selected stages. Later stages may improve or worsen development performance; no monotonic generalisation guarantee follows from reducing a training objective.\n\nIf using early stopping, its feedback data is development data. Passing the final holdout as an early-stopping eval set uses it to choose model complexity. This lab avoids that ambiguity by predeclaring a fixed stage count. More elaborate nested tuning designs follow in Week47.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score, average_precision_score\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["boosting"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprepared = m[:-1].transform(d["X_valid"])\nfor stage, scores in enumerate(m.named_steps["model"].staged_predict_proba(prepared), 1):\n    if stage in [1, 10, 30, 60]:\n        p = scores[:, 1]\n        print("Stage", stage, "validation AUC", round(roc_auc_score(d["y_valid"], p), 3), "AP", round(average_precision_score(d["y_valid"], p), 3))',
  'Verify the final staged probabilities match ordinary pipeline prediction.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["boosting"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nlast = list(m.named_steps["model"].staged_predict_proba(m[:-1].transform(d["X_valid"]))) [-1]\nprint("Final stage matches:", bool(np.allclose(last, m.predict_proba(d["X_valid"]))))',
  'State the proper role of data used for early stopping.',
  'print("Early-stopping feedback selects model complexity")\nprint("Treat that feedback as development, not an untouched final test")',
  [('What does classic binary log-loss boosting combine?', 'Additive decision-score corrections', 'Only an average of hard labels', 'Final-test target values', 'Its sequential score construction differs from forest probability averaging.'),
   ('Does using final labels for early stopping leave the final test untouched?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when few stages are used', 'Those labels influenced the selected model complexity.')],
  'Use the final holdout for early stopping and report it as independent final evidence.', 'Use declared training/development boundaries for every complexity decision.',
  'Validation scores are observed at selected stages of one training fit. The final-stage equality verifies the inference sequence without using final-test outcomes.')

L(5, '3. Run CPU XGBoost with explicit boundaries',
  'Fit the sklearn-compatible classifier and explain its objective and resource settings.',
  [('xgboost-cpu; XGBClassifier', 'The CPU-only package is installed as xgboost-cpu but imported as xgboost. XGBClassifier offers a sklearn-compatible fit/predict_proba interface; no GPU is required here.'),
   ('binary:logistic; hist; n_jobs', 'binary:logistic produces binary probability scores; hist selects histogram-based tree construction. n_jobs=1 bounds teaching-run CPU threading.'),
   ('get_booster; boosted rounds', 'get_booster exposes the fitted native booster. num_boosted_rounds reports its learned iteration count; this exercise uses a predeclared count rather than final-test early stopping.')],
  'XGBoost is another gradient-boosted tree implementation with its own parameters and regularisation choices. A familiar estimator interface does not imply every detail is identical to scikit-learn’s classic GradientBoostingClassifier. Read the objective and configuration you actually run.\n\nThe project pins the tested CPU-only distribution in a fresh environment. It supplies the binary objective, log-loss evaluation metric, histogram construction, learning rate, depth, stage count, seed and one CPU worker. Installation needs internet once; fitting on the local simulator works offline afterwards.\n\nNo final eval_set or early-stopping feedback is supplied. Models and thresholds are compared on validation explicitly in the next part. More sophisticated stopping/tuning is possible, but it must preserve a separate evaluation boundary. A popular library name does not make a model’s score causal, calibrated or better than a simple reference.',
  'import xgboost\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, positive_scores\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["xgboost"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nclassifier = m.named_steps["model"]\nprint("XGBoost version:", xgboost.__version__)\nprint("Objective:", classifier.get_params()["objective"])\nprint("Boosted rounds:", classifier.get_booster().num_boosted_rounds())\nprint("Validation score sample:", positive_scores(m, d["X_valid"].iloc[:4]).round(3).tolist())',
  'Inspect the declared CPU and tree settings without fitting a new model.',
  'from lab import make_candidates\np = make_candidates()["xgboost"].named_steps["model"].get_params()\nfor name in ["tree_method", "n_jobs", "max_depth", "learning_rate", "n_estimators"]:\n    print(name, p[name])',
  'Verify that the fitted positive scores stay within the probability range.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, positive_scores\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["xgboost"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\np = positive_scores(m, d["X_valid"])\nprint("Finite bounded scores:", bool(np.isfinite(p).all() and ((p >= 0) & (p <= 1)).all()))',
  [('Does this exercise require a GPU?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for1200rows', 'It uses the CPU-only distribution and histogram configuration.'),
   ('Does naming eval_metric="logloss" select the final threshold automatically?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a binary objective', 'The action threshold is selected separately by the declared validation cost.')],
  'Assume XGBoost guarantees superiority or feed final labels to its training feedback.', 'Use a declared configuration and compare complete procedures on proper development data.',
  'The fitted native-round count confirms the declared stage count was used. Score range is a structural check, not proof of calibration or useful real customer performance.',
  shell='python -m venv .venv\n.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt\n# The pinned xgboost-cpu distribution imports as xgboost.')

L(5, '4. Select a candidate and threshold with validation evidence',
  'Compare complete procedures and qualify development feature-importance findings.',
  [('select_with_validation', 'The lab fits five predeclared candidates on training, chooses each candidate’s threshold by validation cost and selects the lowest-cost candidate. An exact model-cost tie is broken alphabetically.'),
   ('permutation_importance', 'Shuffle one validation feature and measure deterioration of a declared score. With average-precision scoring, a positive drop means ranking summary worsened. Correlated features can substitute for one another.')],
  'Compare ordinary logistic, balanced logistic, forest, boosting and XGBoost using identical validation cases and cost weights. Each method receives its own validation-selected threshold, so the comparison concerns complete model/decision pairs rather than a shared numeric threshold whose meaning may differ by score scale.\n\nOne validation set guides several choices, so its apparent best cost can overfit development noise. Report that reuse. The selected candidate stays fitted on training only, preserving the score procedure associated with its threshold. No final test outcome is used to rank candidates or adjust the cost matrix.\n\nFeature importance is also development evidence. Permutation importance can describe sensitivity of a chosen ranking score to a shuffled input, but cannot establish a causal cancellation driver or justify a retention intervention. A small effect may reflect correlated substitutes, and repeated feature redesign from the same validation results consumes more development feedback.',
  'from lab import make_splits, select_with_validation\nd = make_splits()\nmodel, winner, comparison = select_with_validation(d)\nfor name, r in comparison.items():\n    print(name, "threshold", round(r["threshold"], 3), "validation cost", r["total_cost"], "precision", round(r["precision"], 3), "recall", round(r["recall"], 3))\nprint("Selected pair:", winner, round(comparison[winner]["threshold"], 3))\nprint("Final test used for selection: False")',
  'Count candidate names and identify the declared model-cost tie-break.',
  'from lab import make_candidates\nprint("Candidate count:", len(make_candidates()))\nprint("Model tie-break: alphabetical name after equal validation cost")',
  'Compute a small validation permutation assessment using average precision, with no final data.',
  TRAIN_LOGISTIC + 'from sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\nfrom lab import FEATURES\nr = permutation_importance(m, d["X_valid"], d["y_valid"], scoring="average_precision", n_repeats=3, random_state=46, n_jobs=1)\nfor name, drop in zip(FEATURES, r.importances_mean):\n    print(name, "validation AP drop", round(float(drop), 4))',
  [('Does the best validation cost guarantee the best final cost?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for a weighted model', 'Selection can exploit development noise and the held-out sample can differ.'),
   ('Does a large permutation effect prove a causal retention intervention?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with average precision', 'Model sensitivity and causal effects are different questions.')],
  'Select by visible final cost or call a validation importance a proven causal driver.', 'Keep selection in development and interpret model sensitivity within its evidence limits.',
  'The comparison calls never pass final labels into selection. The printed winner is the declared validation-cost result, not a post hoc choice from whichever final metric looks best.')

L(5, '5. Report the final result even when selection disappoints',
  'Compare frozen decisions against the baseline and explain the observed final costs honestly.',
  [('run_experiment; final reporting boundary', 'The helper selects on validation, retains the training fit and then evaluates final scores at the selected threshold and0.5. It also reports a never-flag reference on the same final cases.'),
   ('Observed failure to beat a baseline', 'A selected procedure may fail on a predeclared objective. Preserve and explain that result rather than retuning from the final answers or switching to a more flattering metric.')],
  'The final holdout is now inspected. Report confusion counts, accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC AUC, average precision and the declared cost for the same240cases. The selected fit has the same ranking at both thresholds, but its decisions and costs differ.\n\nIn this fixed run, the validation-selected pair costs98illustrative units on the final test, compared with96for never flagging. Its0.5rule costs87, but choosing that rule now because of the final outcome would use test feedback. The selected procedure therefore did not demonstrate improvement on its predeclared final cost objective. Do not hide that behind a stronger AUC or change the seed to manufacture success.\n\nThe final sample has only12actual cancellations, so individual cases have substantial influence. The simulator and assumed costs also limit external claims. Use these results to propose a better development design and new independent evaluation, not to report an already-inspected threshold as newly validated. This is a complete learning experiment even though it does not justify deployment.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\nprint("Selected with validation:", r["winner"], "threshold", round(r["threshold"], 3))\nfor name in ["test_never_flag_baseline", "test_at_half", "test_at_selected_threshold"]:\n    s = r[name]\n    print(name, "TN FP FN TP", [s[k] for k in ["tn", "fp", "fn", "tp"]], "accuracy", round(s["accuracy"], 3), "recall", round(s["recall"], 3), "cost", s["total_cost"])\ngain = r["test_never_flag_baseline"]["total_cost"] - r["test_at_selected_threshold"]["total_cost"]\nprint("Baseline minus selected cost:", gain, "units; negative means no gain")\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(5.4, 7.2))\nfor ax, name, title in zip(axes, ["test_at_half", "test_at_selected_threshold"], ["Same fit at0.5", "Validation-selected threshold"]):\n    s = r[name]\n    matrix = [[s["tn"], s["fp"]], [s["fn"], s["tp"]]]\n    ax.imshow(matrix, cmap="Blues", vmin=0, vmax=240)\n    ax.set(xticks=[0, 1], yticks=[0, 1], xticklabels=["No flag", "Flag"], yticklabels=["Retained", "Cancelled"], xlabel="Decision", ylabel="Actual outcome", title=title + f"; cost {s[\'total_cost\']}")\n    for i in range(2):\n        for j in range(2):\n            ax.text(j, i, str(matrix[i][j]), ha="center", va="center", color="white" if matrix[i][j] > 120 else "black", fontsize=15)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Verify selected and0.5decisions retain the same ranking metrics on the final scores.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\na, b = r["test_at_half"], r["test_at_selected_threshold"]\nprint("Same ROC AUC:", a["roc_auc"] == b["roc_auc"])\nprint("Same average precision:", a["average_precision"] == b["average_precision"])\nprint("Different decision costs:", a["total_cost"], b["total_cost"])',
  'Reconstruct the final selected cost fromFPandFNcounts.',
  'from lab import run_experiment, FP_COST, FN_COST\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\ns = r["test_at_selected_threshold"]\nprint("Recomputed cost:", FP_COST * s["fp"] + FN_COST * s["fn"])\nprint("Reported cost:", s["total_cost"])',
  [('May the test result be hidden because the selected cost missed the baseline?', 'No', 'Yes if AUC is high', 'Only for a synthetic project', 'Honest final reporting includes failure on the declared objective.'),
   ('Is choosing0.5now because its final cost is lower an untouched-test choice?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if the model is unchanged', 'The final labels would have guided the action rule.')],
  'Change the threshold, seed or objective after seeing final failure and report only the nicer result.', 'Preserve the declared experiment and obtain fresh evaluation for later revised claims.',
  'Both matrices use the same240final observations and the same fitted score vector. Their changed decisions illustrate why ranking quality and a chosen action policy can tell different stories.',
  figure=('Final confusion counts at0.5and the validation-selected threshold for the same fitted classifier', 'Rows are actual outcomes and columns are decisions. The selected threshold costs98units versus96for never flagging in this fixed run; this failure is reported rather than hidden.'))

L(5, '6. Milestone: an honest offline classification experiment',
  'Deliver traceable final artifacts, checked inference and a written explanation of limitations.',
  [('write_report; prediction artifacts', 'The lab exports a JSON report and CSV of final row ids, actual labels, positive scores and frozen-threshold flags. Counts and costs can be recalculated from those pairs.'),
   ('predict_checked', 'The supplied interface requires the four named numeric inputs, restores column order, rejects incomplete/invalid values and fractional session/ticket counts, and preserves caller row ids. It does not prove that a new account is inside training support.')],
  'Run the project from docs/python-week46 and inspect both exported files. The report records the simulated-data status, class definition, partitions, candidate selection, threshold grid, cost assumptions, versions, metrics and limitations. The CSV includes every final source row so no difficult outcome disappears from the report.\n\nFor a new fictional snapshot, use the named-input wrapper with the frozen threshold. Its structural checks are useful but cannot verify hidden unit mistakes, actual outcome labels or relevance to a real population. Training median preparation remains a fold-safe example, while the prediction interface deliberately requests complete measurements. A returned flag is a decision, not a verified cancellation or a proven reason to contact someone.\n\nBefore marking completion, explain every confusion cell, calculate precision/recall/F1 by hand, read ROC and PR curves, show why resampling stays inside training, interpret a logistic coefficient on its actual scale and explain the final cost failure. Use the existing notes, quizzes and Previous/Next controls as study checkpoints. Learning is complete when you can defend the workflow, not when a score happens to look impressive.',
  'import json\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom lab import write_report, binary_metrics\nr = write_report("artifacts")\nsaved = json.loads(Path("artifacts/report.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))\nrows = pd.read_csv("artifacts/test-predictions.csv")\nrebuilt = binary_metrics(rows.actual, rows.positive_score, saved["threshold"])\nprint("Final CSV rows:", len(rows))\nprint("Rebuilt confusion counts:", [rebuilt[k] for k in ["tn", "fp", "fn", "tp"]])\nprint("Cost matches JSON:", rebuilt["total_cost"] == r["test_at_selected_threshold"]["total_cost"])\nprint("Recorded limitations:", len(saved["limitations"]))',
  'Predict one fictional named snapshot using a training-only fitted example and preserve its row id.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_candidates, predict_checked, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["logistic"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrow = pd.DataFrame([[12.0, 3, 1, 8.0]], columns=FEATURES, index=["fictional snapshot"])\nprint(predict_checked(m, row, threshold=0.2).round(3).to_string())\nprint("Demonstration threshold, not an evaluated production policy")',
  'Reject a fractional session count with a clear message.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_candidates, predict_checked\nd = make_splits()\nm = make_candidates()["logistic"].fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrow = d["X_valid"].iloc[:1].copy()\nrow["weekly_sessions"] = 2.5\ntry:\n    predict_checked(m, row, threshold=0.2)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(str(error))',
  [('What makes the final cost independently checkable?', 'Paired actual outcomes, flags, threshold and cost rule', 'Only the model library name', 'Only a screenshot of accuracy', 'The numerator and action rule must connect to inspectable evaluated cases.'),
   ('Does a checked new-row flag become a verified customer outcome?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a high score', 'A model output is not observed ground truth.')],
  'Submit only a flattering metric and treat new flags as proven outcomes.', 'Submit the declared workflow, traceable artifacts and honest evidence limits.',
  'The reconstructed CSV counts and cost agree with the report. This links the headline result to every evaluated decision, including the disappointing cases.',
  shell='python lab.py --output artifacts\npython -m pytest -q\npython -m ruff check .\npython -m ruff format --check .')
